    <string name="aiding_wasted">Heard for nothing: %1$s. The receiver holds no orbit for any of those satellites, which is what erasing the aiding data puts right.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_title">Why a position is rejected</string>
    <string name="reason_help_intro">The phone holds every position against several rules and rejects it as soon as the first one speaks. A rejection is not a fault: where the signal is spoofed there are more rejected answers than accepted ones, and that is the work being done properly.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign">What was seen</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means">What it means</string>

    <string name="reason_help_sign_width">The phone named the radius it is somewhere inside, and the radius is over half a kilometre.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_width">Such an answer names a district rather than a place. It is what the towers give while the phone is indoors.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_speed">Reaching this place from the last one in the time between them would have taken over 200 km/h.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_speed">One of the two is false. A spoof throws the marker far off rather than easing it along.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_contradiction">Two different positions arrived carrying the same moment.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_contradiction">The source is contradicting itself, which is what happens when a receiver is caught between a transmitter on the ground and the satellites.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_false_place">The answer landed in an area you marked as somewhere the spoof puts phones.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_false_place">A transmitter puts the marker there, not the satellites. The areas belong to the group, so marking one from any phone is enough.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_unmoved">The movement sensors say the phone was not carried anywhere, and the position changed.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_unmoved">Only a false signal moves the marker of a phone lying still: steps and journeys are counted by a chip of their own that a transmitter cannot reach.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_drifted">The phone is lying where it was put down, and the answers walk away from it a little at a time.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_drifted">A slow drift. A step of thirty metres never trips a comparison between neighbours, but within an hour the marker is somewhere else, so the distance is counted from where the phone was put down.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_hurried">The phone has not been touched, and the answer says it is travelling at the speed of a car.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_hurried">The receiver reports a speed of its own alongside every position, and on a phone lying still that speed is zero. A false signal draws a road and a speed along it, and the speed is inside the single answer — so this catches the first false position rather than the one after it.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_contradicted">The position from the satellites and the position from the towers and the Wi-Fi are kilometres apart.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_contradicted">The towers are harder to imitate than the satellites. This is the one rule that does not compare an answer with an earlier one, so it catches both a slow drift and the very first false answer.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_circling">The answers go round a closed circle at a steady speed.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_circling">Every stretch of such a track reads as ordinary driving. What gives it away is that the road closes, which is how a transmitter draws one and not how anybody drives.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_repeating">The phone has been through this same run of places before.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_repeating">A recorded false route is being played through again.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_one_band">The fix rests on the satellites of the 1575 MHz band — GPS, Galileo, BeiDou — while GLONASS is heard and left out of it.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_one_band">A spoofing transmitter works in one band. GLONASS broadcasts at 1602 MHz, out of its reach, so the receiver could not reconcile it with the satellites it was handed.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_one_transmitter">Every satellite arrives at the same strength however low it claims to be, and the far band is silent.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_one_transmitter">A real sky grows quieter the lower the satellite. One strength for all of them means one point on the ground.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_time_apart">Between two epochs the time the satellites carry advanced by a different amount than the phone\'s own clock did.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_time_apart">The phone counts the seconds since it was switched on, and nothing transmitted can reach that count. A signal built to move the phone has to move the time it carries, and the step shows up here.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_settling">The first five minutes after the phone was switched on, while the answer rests on fewer than four satellites.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_settling">In those minutes the receiver reports places the phone has never been. Answers of that window are held to tighter limits: wider than a hundred metres, or faster than 5 m/s, and they are rejected.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_by_source">You switched this source of a position off in the settings.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_by_source">Nothing is wrong with the answer: it is collected and logged, but it moves no marker and reaches nobody. That is what the switch is for.</string>

    <string name="aiding_title">Aiding data</string>
    <string name="aiding_explained">The orbits and the last known place the receiver searches by. Aiding data gone wrong keeps a whole constellation out of every answer: its satellites are heard and none of them count.</string>
    <string name="aiding_forget">Erase</string>
    <string name="aiding_keep">Leave it</string>
    <string name="aiding_erased">Erased</string>
    <string name="aiding_refused">The receiver would not</string>
    <string name="aiding_confirm_title">Erase the aiding data?</string>
    <string name="aiding_confirm_text">The receiver starts again from nothing: the first position takes minutes instead of seconds, and the orbits are gathered back over the quarter of an hour that follows. Worth doing when a constellation is heard for days without ever entering the answer.</string>

